00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:15.059
Like most places on Earth, Thailand has had
a strange year.

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:21.300
Its largest city, Bangkok, is the world’s
number 1 most visited city, measured by international

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.369
arrivals.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:27.490
As the pandemic spread across the globe, it
felt an even greater impact than other well-traveled

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.339
cities like Paris, London, and Dubai.

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:36.980
Markets, nightclubs, and temples were now,
suddenly, empty, after teeming with foreigners

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.309
for years non-stop.

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:45.770
But only after a brief calm period of strictly-enforced
curfew, were the usual swarm of visitors replaced

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.640
with a new one: pro-democracy protestors.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.660
Thousands of loosely organized students flooded
the streets of Bangkok, defying the emergency

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.899
lockdown orders to incite change long overdue.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:03.710
They ask for the resignation of the Prime
Minister and reforms to the powerful monarchy.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.080
Currently, anyone found to have “defamed,
insulted or threatened” Thai royalty can

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.800
be jailed for years, while the king spends
much of his time relaxing at his villa in

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.080
Germany — paid for by the Thai public.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.740
One of the country’s vocal opposition parties
was legally dissolved by the courts in February.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.540
But while Thailand’s current lack of tourists
is highly unusual, these protests are not

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.950
the least bit.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.900
One of the few consistencies in Thai politics
is their extreme turbulence — the country

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:40.090
has cycled through more governments than almost
anywhere else in the modern world.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:46.310
This timeline shows every coup and attempted
coup since 1910 — roughly twenty-one — though

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:50.950
there have been so many that experts disagree
on the precise number.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.130
Notable is not just the sheer number but also
the frequency.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:02:00.810
The longest a single government has maintained
power is fifteen years, and just twice in

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.740
the entire period.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:09.520
Only three other nations have had more constitutions
— Haiti, Venezuela, and the Dominican Republic.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.640
But there’s a less obvious reason for Thailand’s
instability — one that has nothing to do

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.060
with the monarchy, democracy, or even politics
whatsoever.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.189
 

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:24.400
Looking at a map you can tell a lot about
a country.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.170
Take away all labels, markers, and otherwise
human-generated data, and you still can identify

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.319
the ingredients of a nation:

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:39.480
How big or small it is, which rivers or oceans
it has access to, who its neighbors are, climate,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.909
latitude, forestation, shape, and so on.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:46.220
All of these things can affect a country’s
political stability.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:52.090
A small, European nation situated between
two large powers and landmasses?

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.860
You can expect plenty of invasions.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:00.080
And it’s no surprise that this hunk of snow
has been well protected from attack.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.389
But what you can’t tell from looking at
a map, is a country’s concentration of power.

00:03:05.389 --> 00:03:10.200
Big or small, much more important when it
comes to stability is where decisions get

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.370
made — how many places and how far apart
they are.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:21.129
Of course, in no nation is power truly divided
evenly — it always concentrates in pockets,

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:25.040
just as water droplets naturally attract and
combine.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.389
This is no surprise — Civilization, in general,
develops unevenly.

00:03:30.389 --> 00:03:34.180
Once cities are founded, they experience network
effects.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.430
When there’s a critical mass of energy,
transportation, buildings, and jobs, more

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:44.099
people move, which in turn creates more of
all those things, which, again, attracts more

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:49.370
people, and so on, until they spill over into
neighboring cities or grow to consume them

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.540
entirely.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.950
Still, there are limits.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:58.389
For example, simple geography may support
several centers of power, like LA and New

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:00.849
York on opposite coasts.

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:04.939
The other reason for this growth ceiling is
countervailing forces.

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:10.310
For example, as cities like San Francisco
grow, demand pushes rent to untenable levels,

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.790
forcing an exodus in the opposite direction.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:18.231
So, while a country’s largest city is usually
far bigger than its second -largest, this

00:04:18.231 --> 00:04:23.120
proportion varies based on many factors, like
economic development.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.840
We can see this fact reflected in this list
showing the ratio of a country’s largest

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.010
to second largest city — it’s “primacy
ratio”.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:36.840
Those largest cities which are at least twice
as large as the next largest are said to be

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.820
“primate cities” — they exert outsized
influence on the nation as a whole and represent

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.220
it almost exclusively to the outside world.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:50.610
For example, the greater London area is home
to nearly 10 million inhabitants, far more

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.770
than second place Manchester.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:58.660
Similarly, Mexico City is home to around 9
million people, about 7 million more than

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.260
any other.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:06.240
There are many other examples, including Cairo,
Paris, Jakarta, and Seoul — but, without

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.910
a doubt, the absolute most primate of them
all is Bangkok, Thailand — with at least

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.620
10 million people, depending on where you
draw the metropolitan boundaries.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.520
This dwarfs the country’s second largest
city, which has only 385,000 people.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.700
That’s a ratio of at least 26:1.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:31.830
No other large nation has a population this
unbalanced — and yet, it’s home to nearly

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:37.600
70 million people — meaning almost everyone
lives either around Bangkok, or in one of

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.620
its hundreds of small, distant cities.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:46.220
The capital magnetically concentrates nearly
every major institution, because where else

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.530
would they go?

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:52.950
Bangkok is home to two large airports — one
serving as the hub for international flights,

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:55.940
and the other for shorter regional ones.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:06:00.800
Its fifty districts reportedly grow by as
many as a million people during the day, as

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.920
workers commute from their homes outside the
metropolitan area.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.570
The city is also home to nearly all of Thailand’s
major universities, along with the vast majority

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:11.990
of its hospitals.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:17.020
Altogether, Bangkok contributes 29% of the
national GDP.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.930
In 2010, it accounted for 80% of all urban
land.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.250
How does any of this relate to hourly coups?

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.250
That’s where geography and politics collide…

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:34.970
Because not only are all of Thailand’s museums
and schools here, but also all it’s “action”.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:40.420
It houses the headquarters of all its major
banks, multinational corporations, the national

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:46.870
Stock Exchange, national newspapers, broadcast
media, publishers, and, most importantly,

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:48.710
branches of government.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:54.970
Bangkok singularly holds the country’s monarchy,
military, and bureaucracy — which, in turn,

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:58.810
appoints all provincial governors and district
officers.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:04.260
For obvious reasons, Thailand has greatly
benefitted from this close proximity — indeed,

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:09.720
many nations nurse primate cities as a strategy
for rapid development, hoping to later export

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.150
their work across the country.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:17.010
Every financial or political decision can
be made and executed within a few hour’s

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:22.130
drive — there are no time delays or words
lost in translation.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.170
On the other hand, there’s also a downside.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:30.930
Bangkok is a single, imperative chokepoint,
whose capture or disruption can cause every

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.600
category of chaos across the rest of the nation.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:39.880
And small but symbolic and important, physical
arteries — roads, monuments, and government

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:45.290
buildings, are exactly the sites protestors
seek to occupy and interrupt.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:50.230
The area’s unusually poor city planning
— or, lack thereof — ensures that many

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:55.760
streets are extreme bottlenecks, which, if
blocked, can freeze entire regions.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.720
All of these factors predispose Thailand to
frequent coups — though not necessarily

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.980
successful ones, since after the physically
small territory is captured, it can easily

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.740
fall again.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:13.400
Of course, geography doesn’t solely or even
principally explain Thailand’s instability,

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:18.420
there are other political and structural reasons
which would be foolish to overlook.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:24.880
For example, its mix of democratic institutions
and unchecked royalty creates inevitable tension.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:31.010
But the primacy of Bangkok ensures that, when
they do happen, coups are relatively quick

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:32.680
and decisive.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:37.589
Just as important, the extreme contrast in
living conditions, opportunities, and wealth

00:08:37.589 --> 00:08:42.710
between the nation’s capital and the other
85% or so of the country fuels the desire

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:44.819
for change in the first place.

00:08:44.819 --> 00:08:50.129
In other words, the very same inequalities
which make revolution possible, also motivate

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:55.730
them, as the rural population craves the same
attention and resources as the city.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:09:00.509
While these facts do not necessarily doom
Thailand to an eternity of coups, they make

00:09:00.509 --> 00:09:03.630
real, lasting change a slow effort.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:07.269
Still, progress is very much possible.

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:12.899
The latest round of protests has broken the
decades-long seal on criticizing royalty,

00:09:12.899 --> 00:09:16.139
opening the door to honest and open debate.

00:09:16.139 --> 00:09:21.370
When structural progress is eventually made,
it will be thanks to the hard-fought protests

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:22.819
of today.

00:09:22.819 --> 00:09:26.660
If you’re interested in politics, you’re
probably curious to know where the world is

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:27.689
going next.

00:09:27.689 --> 00:09:32.680
And the best way to predict the future is
with math and logic — two things Brilliant

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.490
